WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.900
Considering the balancing act it has to pull
off, Avengers: Infinity War is pretty great.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.650
The movie has the gargantuan task of advancing
and paying off ten years of storytelling,

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.749
and directors Joe and Anthony Russo handle
the assignment well.

00:00:12.749 --> 00:00:16.950
That said, Infinity War is the first movie
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe that might

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.660
leave casual viewers behind.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.600
If you haven't been keeping up with the franchise,
the film contains a number of moments that

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.730
may leave viewers scratching their heads rather
than fully engrossed.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.840
After 20 movies in this series, it's easy
to lose a few.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.930
And even if you saw them when they came out,
how long's it been since then?

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:35.310
So if you left the theater with questions,
don't worry — we're here to clear up some

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.090
of those muddier moments for you.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:40.430
If you haven't seen the movie, you should
prepare for some spoilers.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:41.880
In your head

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.800
The Mind Stone, implanted in the forehead
of The Vision, is the second Infinity Stone

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.360
we see in the MCU — we see it in the scepter
that Loki wields during The Avengers.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.770
But it's changed hands so many times since
then that it's easy to forget how it ended

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:56.010
up in the powerful android in the first place.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.270
Here's a recap.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:02.820
After Loki is defeated in The Avengers, SHIELD
confiscates the scepter, supposedly for safekeeping.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.361
Unfortunately, the organization turns out
to be rife with undercover HYDRA agents, who

00:01:07.361 --> 00:01:11.150
smuggle the scepter into the hands of their
boss, Wolfgang von Strucker.

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:15.460
Von Strucker uses the stone to perform experiments
on humans and give them powers, which is how

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.790
Quicksilver and Scarlet Witch come to be.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:22.110
During the events of Age of Ultron, The heroes
manage to get the scepter back from von Strucker

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.580
during a raid on his compound.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:28.010
Iron Man realizes it can be used to power
his version of Ultron; unfortunately, Ultron

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:31.911
goes rogue and uses the Mind Stone to power
up a new body for himself.

00:01:31.911 --> 00:01:35.840
The heroes steal both the stone and the robot
body, and then combine the two to bring their

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.540
new teammate, the Vision, to life.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.580
A most unexpected cameo

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.900
Infinity War wouldn't be a Marvel movie without
an out-of-nowhere cameo from Stan Lee, a deep

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.540
cut Marvel Comics character, or someone else
in the movie series that you didn't count

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:49.590
on showing up.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.900
Sometimes they're played for laughs, like
Bruce Banner and Captain America popping up

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.340
in Iron Man 3 and Thor: The Dark World.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.010
But Avengers: Infinity War features a cameo
from a character we were all but certain we'd

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.020
seen the last of: Red Skull.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.180
His appearance to Gamora and Thanos on the
planet Vormir is an awesome moment, but it's

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:09.229
also one that some viewers couldn't help but
respond to with an "Uh, what?"

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:13.310
Red Skull is the villain of 2011's The First
Avenger, where he's obsessed with using the

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.270
Tesseract to fuel HYDRA's plans of world domination.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:20.270
His downfall comes in the film's climax, when
he picks up the cube, which begins to envelop

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.400
him in awesome energy like Indiana Jones'
Ark of the Covenant.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:28.110
With a scream and an explosion, he's shot
out into space with a blue laser beam, seemingly

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.260
never to return.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.980
It's the last we see of the character until
his surprise return in Infinity War.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.890
His tampering with the Space Stone in the
Tesseract led to his imprisonment on the distant

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.970
planet, where he's cursed to guard the Soul
Stone, never able to possess its power or

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:41.970
to leave.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.629
Joe and Anthony Russo have since discussed
the scene's inclusion of Red Skull, explaining,

00:02:45.629 --> 00:02:48.940
"It felt like there was more credibility than
just a random character."

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:53.060
As his ambiguous fate in The First Avenger
demonstrates, the Red Skull's re-emergence

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.170
on Vormir wasn't completely random — it
just might have felt that way.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:58.450
Sibling rivalry

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:02.530
You'd think a father would love his children
equally, but then again, Thanos isn't exactly

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:03.849
father of the year.

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:08.209
Even the daughter he claims to love, Gamora,
is sacrificed by Thanos so he can wield the

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:09.209
Soul Stone.

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:12.170
AND she was kidnapped to begin with — that's
not exactly top-tier parenting.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:16.569
It begs the question as to why Thanos doesn't
seem to harbor the same sort of "affection"

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:17.950
for his other daughter, Nebula.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:22.770
It can also lead to some confusion as to why
Gamora had to be the sacrifice on Vormir when

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.440
Thanos already had Nebula imprisoned on his
ship.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.209
Wouldn't she be a worthy sacrifice for the
Stone as well?

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:32.580
It's established in the Guardians of the Galaxy
films that Thanos raises Nebula and Gamora

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.290
as competitors rather than siblings, pitting
them against one another in battle time after

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.290
time.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:42.300
Gamora constantly wins, and every time she
does, Thanos mutilates Nebula, replacing one

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:47.170
of her limbs or organs with cybernetic appendages
to improve her skills as a warrior.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.019
Nonetheless, she never defeats Gamora.

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:54.239
There's a Darwinian side to Thanos that finds
Nebula to be inferior to Gamora, his masterpiece.

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:57.660
She's the ultimate warrior and he gives himself
the credit for making her that way.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.930
In his own sick way, he truly loves Gamora
in a way he can never love Nebula — not

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.549
that it worked out that well for her.

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:06.049
Paging Captain Marvel

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:10.150
The post-credits scene is a hallmark of the
MCU, and for the most part, they expand the

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.300
universe and set up future films.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.610
Infinity War's post-credits scene is no different.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:18.699
It pulls away from the battle in Wakanda to
show the effects of Thanos erasing half of

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:19.729
all life on Earth.

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:24.659
Nick Fury and Maria Hill realize what's going
on and Fury pages someone as he disintegrates.

00:04:24.659 --> 00:04:27.419
Who could he be reaching out to at a time
like this?

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:30.129
And what does that little red and blue symbol
on his pager mean?

00:04:30.129 --> 00:04:34.150
The moment can be a bit confusing for anybody
not already familiar with the character Captain

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.090
Marvel, who's getting her own solo movie in
March 2019.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:41.210
While not too much is known about how the
character will fit into Avengers 4, we do

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:46.350
know that her solo film takes place in the
'90s, well before the events of most MCU films

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.490
we've seen so far.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.360
We also know a pre-eyepatch Nick Fury will
be involved.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.540
Knowing all this gives the scene a clearer
context: Fury recognizes that something horrible

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.719
is happening and calls on an old friend we
haven't met yet, Captain Marvel, to save the

00:04:58.719 --> 00:04:59.719
day.

00:04:59.719 --> 00:05:01.889
Where she's been, and what she can do to help
in this fight?

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:04.610
That's a question not even the comic books
can answer.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.330
Tony's chest problems

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.430
Iron Man 3 closes with Tony Stark stepping
down as Iron Man, and destroying all of his

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:11.469
suits in the process.

00:05:11.469 --> 00:05:16.389
He also undergoes invasive surgery to remove
the shrapnel in his chest, allowing him to

00:05:16.389 --> 00:05:20.639
finally get rid of the arc reactor that's
kept him alive since the first Iron Man.

00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:25.139
However, Age of Ultron opens with Tony in
the Iron Man suit and back at it full time

00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:26.560
by the time the film is over.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.550
Furthermore, at the beginning of Infinity
War, we see that he's actually still toting

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.539
around an arc reactor on his chest.

00:05:32.539 --> 00:05:33.620
So, why?

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:38.129
As Tony explains to the long-suffering Pepper
Potts, the enclosure spotted in Infinity War

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:41.889
isn't actually an arc reactor of the sort
that was slowly poisoning him in Iron Man

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:42.889
2.

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:46.569
Rather, it's an enclosure for nanotech — basically
a storage space for his newest Iron Man suit

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:47.569
to reside.

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:50.900
He's still clearly got a problem with giving
up the Iron Man identity, but at least he's

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:54.009
stopped poisoning himself with radioactive
gunk.

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:55.419
Where's Wong?

00:05:55.419 --> 00:06:00.289
Doctor Strange and his ally Wong are introduced
pretty spectacularly in Infinity War's first

00:06:00.289 --> 00:06:04.559
massive fight sequence, when the duo join
Iron Man and Spider-Man to take on Thanos'

00:06:04.559 --> 00:06:07.080
henchmen, Cull Obsidian and Ebony Maw.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:11.240
The two sorcerers are a highlight of the first
act, and Wong even gets his own character

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.240
poster.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.110
So why does he vanish after that fight, not
to be seen again for the rest of the film?

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:20.080
The answer lies in Doctor Strange, and is
very briefly alluded to in Infinity War.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.099
It all comes down to Doctor Strange and Wong's
home, the Sanctum Sanctorum.

00:06:24.099 --> 00:06:28.680
Part of a network of Sanctums manned by other
sorcerers, it helps generate a shield against

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.089
evil, otherworldly forces.

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:32.879
There must always be a sorcerer in each Sanctum.

00:06:32.879 --> 00:06:36.999
As Doctor Strange is going to be heading off-planet
with Iron Man and Spider-Man, Wong is left

00:06:36.999 --> 00:06:38.979
to stand guard and keep the planet safe.

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:43.050
We still don't know if he survives Thanos'
purge, but we can at least rest assured that

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.990
he kept Earth safe from non-Thanos threats
while the rest of the Avengers were trying

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:48.599
to stop the big purple guy.

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:51.059
Who had the Infinity Gauntlet?

00:06:51.059 --> 00:06:55.479
The Infinity Stones and the gauntlet build
to bear them have been at play in the MCU

00:06:55.479 --> 00:06:59.379
since its earliest days, even if only in the
form of brief cameos.

00:06:59.379 --> 00:07:03.929
When all six stones rest in the Infinity Gauntlet,
the user is granted complete control over

00:07:03.929 --> 00:07:04.929
the universe.

00:07:04.929 --> 00:07:09.229
The Gauntlet actually appears in the franchise
before any of the Stones do, glimpsed briefly

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:11.129
in Odin's trophy room in Thor.

00:07:11.129 --> 00:07:15.699
However, according to Infinity War, Thanos
gets the gauntlet from Eitri, a dwarf and

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:17.009
master weapons forger.

00:07:17.009 --> 00:07:18.259
How does this make sense?

00:07:18.259 --> 00:07:22.039
It's quite simple, really: the Infinity Gauntlet
in Asgard was counterfeit.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:26.140
This is revealed very quickly in Thor: Ragnarok,
when Hela ransacks the trophy room.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:27.140
"Fake.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.280
Most of this stuff in here is fake."

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:35.939
As it turns out, the real Gauntlet is with
Thanos, who had Eitri build him a real one,

00:07:35.939 --> 00:07:37.039
as the dwarf describes.

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:41.219
It might be a bit confusing if you miss Hela's
line in Ragnarok, so let's not even talk about

00:07:41.219 --> 00:07:43.550
that third Gauntlet in Eitri's studio.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.110
Drax's vengeance

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:49.309
Drax the Destroyer, a character who has largely
served as comic relief over the course of

00:07:49.309 --> 00:07:53.800
his last two cinematic outings, has one of
the most tragic backstories in the MCU.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:57.909
As the Guardians of the Galaxy movies establish,
his wife and daughter are victims of the wrath

00:07:57.909 --> 00:08:00.209
of the Kree terrorist Ronan the Accuser.

00:08:00.209 --> 00:08:04.840
The title of Destroyer was earned thanks to
the murderous rampage he's been on since losing

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:05.840
them.

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:09.089
It does make one wonder, though, why he holds
such a grudge against Thanos if Ronan is the

00:08:09.089 --> 00:08:10.279
one who murdered his family.

00:08:10.279 --> 00:08:15.120
Here's why: After Ronan is defeated in Guardians
of the Galaxy, Drax learns that the Kree warrior

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:19.470
has been working for Thanos all along, which
means Thanos is ultimately responsible for

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.330
the death of Drax's family.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:25.619
Drax then refocuses his thirst for vengeance
on the Mad Titan, and carries that rage into

00:08:25.619 --> 00:08:26.830
Infinity War.

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:28.879
"What did it cost?"

00:08:28.879 --> 00:08:32.979
Most MCU films are pretty straightforward,
but this changes for a moment in Avengers:

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:34.000
Infinity War.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.229
After Thanos fulfills his goal and wipes out
half of all life with the snap of his fingers,

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:41.079
the film cuts to Thanos and Gamora in an unknown
world.

00:08:41.079 --> 00:08:45.339
Between Gamora's young appearance and the
inexplicable change in location, this segment

00:08:45.339 --> 00:08:46.500
is a bit unclear.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.500
Is it all in Thanos' head?

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:48.720
Why is Gamora a child?

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.019
What does it mean?

00:08:50.019 --> 00:08:53.160
What we're seeing in this moment has been
confirmed to be a realm existing inside the

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:54.160
Soul Stone.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.240
In the comics, this place is Soulworld, and
it's populated by the souls of those absorbed

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.240
by the Soul Gem.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:03.300
While there's yet to be an explanation for
the Soulworld in the MCU, we at least know

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.920
that's probably where Thanos' conversation
with Gamora takes place, and she appears as

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:09.160
a child because it's how he still envisions
her.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:12.660
This may go on to be a crucial scene once
Avengers 4 arrives.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:17.180
Maybe Ant-Man and the Wasp will shrink inside
the Soul Stone to bust everyone out, if they're

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:21.780
actually in there — or, maybe Captain Marvel
will just beat down Thanos until he lets everyone

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:22.340
go.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:23.700
We'll all find out in 2019.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:25.140
Thanks for watching!

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:28.210
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.279
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

